ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":738.3676554361979,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":228.0,"r":356.6666666666667,"t":747.0343221028646},"charspan":[0,38],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"JH Kim et al Carbon 101 (2016) 449 457"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"respectively (Fig. 2a,b and Table 1). With CO-stripping; consistent results were obtained. The ECSAco of PtJCB and PtlCB_O decreased from 79.8 m2 gpt' to 57.1 m2 gpt' and 117.8 m2 gPt' to 53.4 m2 gPt retaining 71.5% and 45.3% of their initial ECSAs, respectively (Fig: 2c,d and Table 1). The better durability of the Pt/CB was more clearly evidenced by the kinetic data  obtained after the ADTs decreased by 12 mV (from 0.904 V to 0.892 V) after the ADT, Pt| CB_0 underwent more significant  decrease of 23 mV (from 0.912 V to 0.889 V). In addition, the PtJCB preserved 67.4% of the initial kinetic current density and mass activity after the ADT; which is ference in the durability between PtJCB and PtJCB_O appears to arise from the  different degrees of agglomeration of Pt nano particles. TEM images of the two catalysts after the ADTs (Fig. 3) from clearly revealed that the Pt/CB_O underwent more severe agglomeration during the durability tests; the size of Pt nanoparticles in PtJCB_0 (3.5 nm) was larger than that in PtJCB (2.7 nm)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":570.7009887695312,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":30.666666666666668,"r":286.3333333333333,"t":728.7009887695312},"charspan":[0,853],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":696.0343221028646,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":300.0,"r":553.6666666666666,"t":729.0343221028646},"charspan":[854,1040],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"respectively (Fig. 2a,b and Table 1). With CO-stripping; consistent results were obtained. The ECSAco of PtJCB and PtlCB_O decreased from 79.8 m2 gpt' to 57.1 m2 gpt' and 117.8 m2 gPt' to 53.4 m2 gPt retaining 71.5% and 45.3% of their initial ECSAs, respectively (Fig: 2c,d and Table 1). The better durability of the Pt/CB was more clearly evidenced by the kinetic data  obtained after the ADTs decreased by 12 mV (from 0.904 V to 0.892 V) after the ADT, Pt| CB_0 underwent more significant  decrease of 23 mV (from 0.912 V to 0.889 V). In addition, the PtJCB preserved 67.4% of the initial kinetic current density and mass activity after the ADT; which is ference in the durability between PtJCB and PtJCB_O appears to arise from the  different degrees of agglomeration of Pt nano particles. TEM images of the two catalysts after the ADTs (Fig. 3) from clearly revealed that the Pt/CB_O underwent more severe agglomeration during the durability tests; the size of Pt nanoparticles in PtJCB_0 (3.5 nm) was larger than that in PtJCB (2.7 nm)"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"To identify molecular-level factors responsible for the ADT results, the two catalysts were studied by XPS, in situ XANES, and ICP MS. The Pt 4f XPS spectra of the two catalysts (Fig. 4) showed doublet peaks at 71.8 eV and 75.2 eV_ which can be deconvoluted into three peaks. The deconvoluted doublet peaks at 71.5 eV and 75.0 eV, 72.8 eVand 76.3 eV,and 74 eVand 77.5 eV can be ascribed to the Pto , PtO; and PtO2 phases, respectively [52] We quantified the integrated areas of deconvoluted peaks in Pt 4f XPS spectra; and the results are presented in Table 2. The area corresponding to PtO2 phase in the Pt/CB is 17.5%, and that in the Pt/CB_O is increased to area for Pt is reduced from 53.2% becomes more peak","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":573.0343221028646,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":300.3333333333333,"r":555.0,"t":698.3676554361979},"charspan":[0,712],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"To identify molecular-level factors responsible for the ADT results, the two catalysts were studied by XPS, in situ XANES, and ICP MS. The Pt 4f XPS spectra of the two catalysts (Fig. 4) showed doublet peaks at 71.8 eV and 75.2 eV_ which can be deconvoluted into three peaks. The deconvoluted doublet peaks at 71.5 eV and 75.0 eV, 72.8 eVand 76.3 eV,and 74 eVand 77.5 eV can be ascribed to the Pto , PtO; and PtO2 phases, respectively [52] We quantified the integrated areas of deconvoluted peaks in Pt 4f XPS spectra; and the results are presented in Table 2. The area corresponding to PtO2 phase in the Pt/CB is 17.5%, and that in the Pt/CB_O is increased to area for Pt is reduced from 53.2% becomes more peak"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"this figure can be viewed online )","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":49.03432210286462,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":31.333333333333332,"r":132.66666666666666,"t":58.36765543619788},"charspan":[0,34],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"this figure can be viewed online )"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(a)","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":528.367655436198,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":136.66666666666666,"r":156.0,"t":544.3676554361979},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"(a)"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(d)","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":527.7009887695312,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":296.6666666666667,"r":316.6666666666667,"t":543.7009887695312},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"(d)"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"20 nm","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":398.36765543619794,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":137.33333333333334,"r":164.0,"t":408.36765543619794},"charspan":[0,5],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"20 nm"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"20 nm","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":395.70098876953125,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":297.6666666666667,"r":325.3333333333333,"t":408.03432210286456},"charspan":[0,5],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"